      "text": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nWe shipped a feature nobody used.\n\nNot “low adoption.” Not “needs better onboarding.”\n\nNobody used it.\n\nThe team had spent weeks building it. The request had come up in customer conversations. It looked valuable in planning. The design was polished, the implementation was solid, and we announced it with confidence.\n\nThen we watched the data.\n\nSilence.\n\nOur first instinct was to fix the feature:\n\n- Improve the UI  \n- Add tooltips  \n- Send an email announcement  \n- Put it in the product tour  \n\nBut the real issue wasn’t discoverability.\n\nIt was relevance.\n\nWe had heard customers describe a problem and immediately translated it into a solution. We never slowed down to understand:\n\n- How often the problem actually occurred  \n- What they did instead  \n- Whether it was painful enough to change behavior  \n- Who specifically needed the solution  \n- What tradeoff they would make to use it  \n\nThe feature solved a problem in theory, but not one important enough in practice.\n\nThe lesson: customer requests are not product requirements.\n\nA request is evidence. It’s a starting point for discovery—not a roadmap item.\n\nNow, before committing to a feature, we ask a different set of questions:\n\n1. What job is the customer trying to get done?  \n2. What is their current workaround?  \n3. What does that workaround cost them?  \n4. How many customers have this problem, and how often?  \n5. What is the smallest test we can run before building?  \n\nSometimes the best outcome of product discovery is deciding not to build.\n\nThat can feel disappointing when a team is eager to ship. But avoiding months of work on something customers won’t adopt is a win—not a failure.\n\nShipping teaches you what works.\n\nBut listening before shipping teaches you what’s worth building.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nWe spent 4 months building a feature that 11 people used.\n\nNot 11 percent. Eleven people. Total.\n\nThe feature was customizable reporting dashboards. Sales kept hearing \"we need better reporting\" on calls. It showed up in every churn survey. The roadmap committee greenlit it fast — this was clearly what customers wanted.\n\nWe did everything \"right.\" Specced it carefully. Built it well. Launched with in-app announcements, email campaigns, even a webinar.\n\nWeek one: a few hundred clicks. Mostly curiosity.\nMonth three: 11 weekly active users. Out of 40,000 accounts.\n\nThe post-mortem was uncomfortable. Because here's the truth: customers weren't lying when they said they wanted better reporting. They did want it — in the abstract. But wanting a feature and using a feature are separated by a canyon of friction: setup time, learning curve, breaking an existing habit.\n\nWhat they actually wanted wasn't \"customizable dashboards.\" It was the answer to one specific question their boss asks every Monday. We handed them a toolkit. They wanted an answer.\n\nThe lesson I carry with me now:\n\n**\"Customers asked for it\" is not validation. It's a hypothesis.**\n\nSaying yes to a feature costs a customer nothing. Using it costs them time, attention, and behavior change. The gap between stated preference and revealed preference is where roadmaps go to die.\n\nWhat I do differently today:\n\n1. **Before building, I ask: what do users do *today* to solve this?** If the answer is \"nothing\" or \"they just live with it,\" that's a warning sign, not an opportunity.\n\n2. **I test with the cheapest possible version.** A manual workaround. A spreadsheet. A fake door. If people won't use the clunky version, they won't use the polished one either.\n\n3. **When someone requests a feature, I dig for the problem underneath.** \"I need better reporting\" becomes \"Tell me about the last time reporting failed you.\" The story is always more useful than the request.\n\nAnd those 11 users? They love the feature. Deeply. It's core to their workflow. We just built a power tool for a niche while believing we were building a staple for everyone.\n\nThat distinction — who it's actually for, and how many of them exist — is worth more than any roadmap meeting I've ever sat in.\n\nThe most expensive code isn't the code that breaks. It's the code that runs perfectly, serving no one.\n\nHave you ever shipped something nobody used? What did it teach you?\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
